1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:03,920
You've spent the last few lessons learning who you are, what

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:07,560
strengths you bring, and why different people make a team stronger.

3
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:11,880
Now, we can name the skill that turns those ideas into

4
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:13,460
action: leadership.

5
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:17,300
The source curriculum gives us a simple working definition.

6
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:20,840
Leadership is directing people toward a goal.

7
00:00:21,540 --> 00:00:23,840
Notice what that definition does not say.

8
00:00:24,190 --> 00:00:28,000
It does not say having a title, being the loudest person,

9
00:00:28,420 --> 00:00:29,540
or giving orders.

10
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:34,180
Leadership is about helping people move toward something they are trying

11
00:00:34,180 --> 00:00:34,840
to achieve.

12
00:00:35,460 --> 00:00:38,780
For a community leader, the first question is not, am I

13
00:00:38,780 --> 00:00:39,200
important?

14
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:43,020
It is, can I help people move toward a useful goal?

15
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:46,040
The curriculum also makes an important point.

16
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:50,080
Leadership is not reserved for one special personality.

17
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:53,920
Its message is that leadership can be acquired and developed.

18
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:58,200
Some people may feel naturally comfortable taking the lead, while others

19
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:01,920
build that ability through experience, practice, and responsibility.

20
00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:06,060
That means you should not decide, I'm just not a leader,

21
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:09,960
because you are quiet, careful, creative, or people focused.

22
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:12,900
Those traits can all appear in leadership.

23
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:17,360
The better question is, how do I naturally influence a group,

24
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:20,340
and what leadership skills do I still need to strengthen?

25
00:01:21,140 --> 00:01:25,360
Community leadership grows through practice, not through waiting for a title.

26
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,940
Leadership can also look different from one situation to another.

27
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:34,500
Some people lead by giving clear direction and making fast decisions.

28
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:37,640
Others persuade people around an idea.

29
00:01:38,380 --> 00:01:42,940
Some motivate a team, while others become role models through consistent

30
00:01:42,940 --> 00:01:44,860
actions and the way they treat people.

31
00:01:45,580 --> 00:01:49,340
None of those methods automatically makes one person more of a

32
00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:50,080
leader than another.

33
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:55,280
The source curriculum wants you to recognize that different situations need

34
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:56,260
different strengths.

35
00:01:57,040 --> 00:02:01,020
A useful leader learns how they tend to lead, understands how

36
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:04,560
other people lead, and chooses the approach that helps the group

37
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:07,420
reach the goal without ignoring the people involved.

38
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:12,200
The curriculum uses a teamwork video with penguins, ants,

39
00:02:12,380 --> 00:02:15,500
and other animals to make one leadership lesson visible.

40
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:18,880
The strength of a group can be greater than the strength

41
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:20,020
of one individual.

42
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:23,720
Pause the video and watch the linked teamwork clip.

43
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:27,480
When you return, focus on one question.

44
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:30,520
What made the smaller individuals successful?

45
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:34,600
The lesson is not that everyone must act the same.

46
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:38,380
It is that people become more effective when they understand their

47
00:02:38,380 --> 00:02:41,200
abilities, cooperate, and combine strengths.

48
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:45,240
A community leader should develop personal skills, but

49
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:48,660
also know how to bring different people together so those skills

50
00:02:48,660 --> 00:02:49,720
can support one another.

51
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:54,300
Now you'll use the official leadership type assessment provided with this

52
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:54,640
lesson.

53
00:02:55,300 --> 00:02:58,900
The source explains that the assessment contains groups of traits that

54
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:02,400
you rank according to how strongly they describe you, then total

55
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:05,800
the results to see which leadership patterns appear most strongly.

56
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:09,520
Follow the instructions on the ACT worksheet exactly.

57
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:11,980
Do not try to force the result you want.

58
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:15,540
Pause the video and complete the assessment honestly.

59
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:20,280
Treat the result as information about your current tendencies, not as

60
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:21,100
a permanent label.

61
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:25,280
The next lesson will explain the four leadership directions and help

62
00:03:25,280 --> 00:03:28,000
you understand what your result actually means in practice.

63
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:32,580
Before you leave the assessment, remember what a result can

64
00:03:32,580 --> 00:03:34,240
and cannot tell you.

65
00:03:34,740 --> 00:03:38,600
It can help you notice patterns in how you think, communicate,

66
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:40,940
decide, or work with people.

67
00:03:41,420 --> 00:03:45,520
It cannot define your whole identity, and it does not

68
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:48,640
prove that one leadership style is better than another.

69
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:53,920
The source is very clear that different leadership types matter because

70
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:56,560
different situations need different qualities.

71
00:03:57,460 --> 00:04:01,020
A result should create self-awareness, not a box.

72
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:05,280
If two patterns seem close, that is not a problem.

73
00:04:05,880 --> 00:04:10,140
Human behavior changes across roles and situations, and you

74
00:04:10,140 --> 00:04:13,500
will understand that better when we build the leadership compass in

75
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:14,140
the next lesson.

76
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:18,680
There's one more source activity worth keeping because it gives you

77
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:21,020
a different kind of evidence about yourself.

78
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:25,600
Choose five people whose opinions you trust and ask each

79
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:26,880
one two questions.

80
00:04:27,480 --> 00:04:31,300
What are my strengths and what positive traits do you see

81
00:04:31,300 --> 00:04:31,580
in me?

82
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:36,300
Your goal is to collect several specific answers, including repeated

83
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:36,580
ones.

84
00:04:37,060 --> 00:04:40,580
If the same strength appears more than once, that may be

85
00:04:40,580 --> 00:04:41,840
a useful pattern to notice.

86
00:04:42,540 --> 00:04:46,700
But remember, these answers are opinions, not final facts

87
00:04:46,700 --> 00:04:47,540
about who you are.

88
00:04:48,220 --> 00:04:50,880
Pause now to write the names of the five people you

89
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:54,380
will ask, then complete the conversations before the next lesson.

90
00:04:54,980 --> 00:04:59,120
Why ask other people when this unit is about self-awareness?

91
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:03,240
Because leaders need both an internal view and an external

92
00:05:03,240 --> 00:05:03,660
one.

93
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:08,520
You know your intentions, but other people experience your behavior.

94
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:13,220
Their feedback can reveal strengths you overlook, or show that a

95
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:16,460
quality you value is visible consistently to others.

96
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,220
At the same time, one person's opinion, especially a negative

97
00:05:21,220 --> 00:05:23,240
one, does not define you.

98
00:05:23,780 --> 00:05:27,820
Use feedback as information to examine, not a verdict to

99
00:05:27,820 --> 00:05:28,080
obey.

100
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:31,620
The curriculum connects this research with development.

101
00:05:32,220 --> 00:05:34,900
Discover your strengths so you can invest in them.

102
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:39,380
Keep developing undiscovered abilities, and become more useful to the

103
00:05:39,380 --> 00:05:41,160
people and projects around you.

104
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:44,720
Today, you learned that leadership is a skill that can be

105
00:05:44,720 --> 00:05:48,500
developed, not one personality you either have or do not have.

106
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:53,900
You learned that people can lead through direction, influence, motivation, example,

107
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:57,840
and teamwork, and that knowing your own tendencies helps you contribute

108
00:05:57,840 --> 00:05:58,620
more deliberately.

109
00:05:59,120 --> 00:06:03,340
You also completed a leadership assessment, and began collecting outside feedback

110
00:06:03,340 --> 00:06:04,200
about your strengths.

111
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:05,780
Keep both results.

112
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:09,260
In the next lesson, we place those patterns onto the leadership

113
00:06:09,260 --> 00:06:13,460
compass, explore four different leadership directions, and ask a question

114
00:06:13,460 --> 00:06:15,740
every community leader needs to answer.

115
00:06:16,360 --> 00:06:19,220
When is my natural style useful, and when do I need

116
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:19,680
to adapt?
